val studyTopics = listOf(
Topic("Order of instructions", Category.RULES, "Stop as directed by the officer.\n\nA police officer's directions take precedence over traffic signals, signs and general rules.\n\nRemember: The officer's instruction has higher priority than the light.\n\nRemember: The police instruction is sufficient.\n\nRemember: A clear road does not cancel the instruction.", 8),
Topic("Giving way", Category.RULES, "Slow down, check carefully and stop only if necessary.\n\nGiving way requires you to avoid obstructing intersecting traffic; a complete stop is not automatic.\n\nRemember: A give-way sign does not impose a fixed stop.\n\nRemember: You must clearly demonstrate readiness to yield.\n\nRemember: The duty covers traffic from both directions.", 23),
Topic("Stop line", Category.RULES, "Immediately before the stop line.\n\nA stop sign requires a complete stop even when the road appears empty.\n\nRemember: The marked stop line determines the stopping point.\n\nRemember: That puts the car into conflicting traffic.\n\nRemember: The stop obligation is unconditional.", 24),
Topic("Queue at a stop sign", Category.RULES, "Make your own complete stop at the line.\n\nEach driver must fulfil the stop obligation at the proper stopping point.\n\nRemember: The other driver's stop does not fulfil your obligation.\n\nRemember: The obligation applies even without traffic.\n\nRemember: A horn is not a substitute for stopping.", 24),
Topic("Priority to the right", Category.RULES, "Vehicles approaching from your right.\n\nThe priority-to-the-right rule applies when no sign, signal or other priority rule replaces it.\n\nRemember: This reverses the rule.\n\nRemember: Vehicle size does not establish priority.\n\nRemember: Road width alone does not change the rule.", 26),
Topic("Different road widths", Category.RULES, "You give way to it.\n\nA wider road is not automatically a priority road.\n\nRemember: Width alone is not a priority rule.\n\nRemember: Going straight does not override priority to the right.\n\nRemember: Speed never creates priority.", 28),
Topic("Open parking areas", Category.RULES, "Give way to the vehicle on your right.\n\nPriority to the right can apply in open areas as well as conventional junctions.\n\nRemember: That is not the general rule.\n\nRemember: Markings are not necessary for the rule.\n\nRemember: A horn cannot establish priority.", 30),
Topic("Leaving a property", Category.RULES, "Give way to road users whose paths you cross.\n\nLeaving a property is covered by the exit rule.\n\nRemember: The exit rule is broader than priority to the right.\n\nRemember: The turn does not remove the exit obligation.\n\nRemember: Starting first does not establish priority.", 35),
Topic("Crossing an uninterrupted cycle path", Category.RULES, "Give way on both the cycle path and the road.\n\nThe exit rule applies to the path you cross and the road you enter.\n\nRemember: Cyclists on the uninterrupted path also have protection.\n\nRemember: Cars do not have general priority over cyclists.\n\nRemember: It is a route for cyclists, not parking.", 35),
Topic("Turning left", Category.RULES, "Let the oncoming car pass before turning.\n\nA round green light may also be shown to oncoming traffic; turning duties still apply.\n\nRemember: Green does not cancel the turning rule.\n\nRemember: An indicator communicates intention, not priority.\n\nRemember: It is your turn that crosses its path.", 33),
Topic("Blocked junction", Category.RULES, "Wait before entering until you can clear the junction.\n\nYou must avoid becoming stationary where you obstruct crossing traffic.\n\nRemember: Green does not authorise blocking the junction.\n\nRemember: That obstructs other traffic.\n\nRemember: The crossing must also be kept clear.", 38),
Topic("Bus leaving a stop", Category.RULES, "Slow down and let the bus leave.\n\nAt a speed limit of 50 km/h or lower, drivers in the affected lane must allow a signalling bus out.\n\nRemember: That frustrates the bus rule.\n\nRemember: The 50 km/h rule applies here.\n\nRemember: That introduces a new danger.", 39),
Topic("Bus on a faster road", Category.RULES, "The bus must give way, but you should still be ready to cooperate.\n\nThe special obligation to let a bus out applies at limits of 50 km/h or lower.\n\nRemember: The limit is above the bus-rule threshold.\n\nRemember: All drivers still owe care and consideration.\n\nRemember: Its driver must avoid danger.", 39),
Topic("Amber light", Category.RULES, "Stop before the line.\n\nAmber means stop unless you are so close that stopping safely is no longer possible.\n\nRemember: Amber is not an invitation to speed up.\n\nRemember: An empty junction does not remove the stop requirement.\n\nRemember: That can make the stop unsafe or too late.", 40),
Topic("Flashing amber", Category.RULES, "Use the applicable signs or general priority rules with extra caution.\n\nFlashing amber warns that normal signal control is not operating.\n\nRemember: It grants no general right to proceed.\n\nRemember: Priority must come from signs or rules.\n\nRemember: Pedestrian duties still apply.", 40),
Topic("Red and amber together", Category.RULES, "Remain stopped; green is about to appear.\n\nThe combined signal still requires you to stop.\n\nRemember: The signal has not yet permitted movement.\n\nRemember: There is no general right turn on red.\n\nRemember: The signal applies to the drivers facing it.", 41),
Topic("Uncontrolled pedestrian crossing", Category.RULES, "Clearly slow down and give way.\n\nDrivers must give way to pedestrians on or about to enter an uncontrolled crossing.\n\nRemember: The duty begins before they reach your lane.\n\nRemember: A horn does not replace giving way.\n\nRemember: The crossing rule protects all pedestrians.", 46),
Topic("Walking a bicycle", Category.RULES, "As a pedestrian.\n\nA person walking with a bicycle is a pedestrian rather than a riding cyclist.\n\nRemember: A pushed bicycle is not a motor vehicle.\n\nRemember: They are walking rather than riding.\n\nRemember: Pushing a bicycle is allowed.", 46),
Topic("Bicycle passage on a straight road", Category.RULES, "Adapt speed so you do not endanger cyclists already on the passage.\n\nThe cyclist's obligation to give way does not remove the driver's duty to avoid danger.\n\nRemember: Drivers still have a safety obligation.\n\nRemember: No signal is present.\n\nRemember: Deliberately creating danger is unacceptable.", 50),
Topic("Bicycle passage after a turn", Category.RULES, "Drive slowly and give the cyclist an opportunity to pass.\n\nTurning drivers and drivers exiting a roundabout have additional duties at bicycle passages.\n\nRemember: Indicating does not remove the obligation.\n\nRemember: The turning situation has additional duties.\n\nRemember: That obstructs the cyclist's route.", 50),
Topic("Bicycle crossing", Category.RULES, "You must give way to the cyclist.\n\nA bicycle crossing has an explicit driver duty to yield to cyclists using or about to use it.\n\nRemember: Vehicle size does not establish priority.\n\nRemember: This is a bicycle crossing.\n\nRemember: Dismounting is not required at a bicycle crossing.", 52),
Topic("Entering a roundabout", Category.RULES, "All vehicles already circulating in it.\n\nThe entry obligation applies to circulating vehicles, including bicycles.\n\nRemember: Roundabout entry has its own rule.\n\nRemember: Bicycles are vehicles too.\n\nRemember: Your intended exit does not remove the duty.", 58),
Topic("Leaving a roundabout", Category.RULES, "Indicate right.\n\nAn exit is a movement to the right and must be signalled.\n\nRemember: That gives the wrong exit message.\n\nRemember: Hazard lights do not indicate a normal exit.\n\nRemember: You still need to signal the exit.", 58),
Topic("Changing roundabout lanes", Category.RULES, "Check and change lanes only without danger or obstruction.\n\nBeing in the inner lane does not give priority over traffic in the outer lane.\n\nRemember: Lane changes must be safe.\n\nRemember: Indicating does not create a gap.\n\nRemember: An abrupt stop can cause a collision; continue around if needed.", 58),
Topic("Stopping in a tunnel", Category.RULES, "No; continue to a suitable permitted stopping place.\n\nVoluntary stopping and parking in a tunnel are prohibited.\n\nRemember: Hazard lights do not legalise a voluntary stop.\n\nRemember: Duration does not create an exception.\n\nRemember: Engine status does not change the restriction.", 68),
Topic("Before a pedestrian crossing", Category.RULES, "No, stopping and parking are prohibited within 10 metres before it.\n\nA vehicle there can hide pedestrians from approaching drivers.\n\nRemember: Remaining inside does not remove the prohibition.\n\nRemember: The distance rule still applies.\n\nRemember: Vehicle size does not remove the rule.", 69),
Topic("Waiting for a passenger", Category.RULES, "Parking.\n\nWaiting is parking when no actual boarding, alighting, loading or unloading is taking place.\n\nRemember: Waiting for a person is not loading goods.\n\nRemember: A running engine does not change the definition.\n\nRemember: The traffic situation did not require you to wait.", 70),
Topic("Priority-road parking", Category.RULES, "No.\n\nParking on a priority road is generally prohibited unless parking is specifically permitted.\n\nRemember: A shoulder alone is not permission.\n\nRemember: The ordinary parking time rule does not override this prohibition.\n\nRemember: Lights improve visibility but do not create permission.", 71),
Topic("Level-crossing parking", Category.RULES, "Parking is prohibited within 30 metres of the crossing.\n\nThe parking restriction extends 30 metres on either side of a railway or tramway crossing.\n\nRemember: The restriction extends beyond the rails.\n\nRemember: Train visibility does not change the distance rule.\n\nRemember: The restriction also applies to cars.", 71),
Topic("One-way parking", Category.RULES, "Either side.\n\nOne-way streets are an exception to the usual right-side stopping and parking rule.\n\nRemember: One-way streets allow the left side too.\n\nRemember: Parking in the middle would obstruct traffic.\n\nRemember: One-way status does not permit pavement parking.", 72),
Topic("Date parking", Category.RULES, "The side with even house numbers.\n\nDuring the restriction's hours, the date and prohibited side have the same parity.\n\nRemember: That reverses the date-parking rule.\n\nRemember: Check the sign's times; the rule identifies one side.\n\nRemember: Even dates are covered by the rule.", 73),
Topic("Parking time panels", Category.RULES, "Weekdays before Sundays and public holidays.\n\nParentheses cover the day before a Sunday or public holiday, not just ordinary Saturdays.\n\nRemember: Sundays and public holidays normally use red times.\n\nRemember: Ordinary weekday times are shown without parentheses.\n\nRemember: School holidays do not define these times.", 73),
Topic("Bus-stop space", Category.RULES, "20 metres before and 5 metres after the stop sign.\n\nThe larger approach space allows a bus to enter its stop.\n\nRemember: That reverses the distances.\n\nRemember: Those are not the bus-stop distances.\n\nRemember: The protected area extends beyond the sign.", 74),
Topic("Picking up at a bus stop", Category.RULES, "Only if you do not hinder bus or tram traffic.\n\nActual boarding and alighting may be allowed, but the stop must remain usable by public transport.\n\nRemember: You must not hinder the bus.\n\nRemember: Goods unloading is not the passenger exception.\n\nRemember: A limited passenger exception exists.", 74),
Topic("Motorway exit missed", Category.RULES, "Continue to the next suitable exit.\n\nReversing or turning around on a motorway is prohibited.\n\nRemember: Reversing is prohibited even on the shoulder.\n\nRemember: Such openings are not for ordinary U-turns.\n\nRemember: Stopping and walking on the motorway are not permitted for this purpose.", 91),
Topic("Acceleration lane", Category.RULES, "Adapt your speed and cooperate with traffic to merge safely.\n\nBoth entering and existing traffic must show consideration and adapt.\n\nRemember: The lane does not grant absolute priority.\n\nRemember: Stopping is not the normal merging method.\n\nRemember: Mirrors alone can miss nearby vehicles.", 91),
Topic("Motorway eligibility", Category.RULES, "No, mopeds are prohibited.\n\nThe design-speed requirement does not override the specific ban on mopeds.\n\nRemember: Mopeds are explicitly excluded.\n\nRemember: The shoulder is not a moped route.\n\nRemember: The vehicle prohibition still applies.", 91),
Topic("Overtaking at a crossing", Category.RULES, "No.\n\nOvertaking there can hide pedestrians and is prohibited.\n\nRemember: A horn does not create an exception.\n\nRemember: Its speed does not remove the prohibition.\n\nRemember: A broken line does not override the crossing restriction.", 99),
Topic("Overtaking speed", Category.RULES, "No.\n\nThe speed limit still applies during an overtake.\n\nRemember: There is no general overtaking allowance.\n\nRemember: Duration does not legalise speeding.\n\nRemember: Another driver's agreement cannot change the limit.", 101),
Topic("Railway priority", Category.RULES, "Give way to the train.\n\nYou must check for trains and yield even if warning equipment is not operating.\n\nRemember: The absence of a red signal is not a guarantee.\n\nRemember: A train cannot readily stop or swerve.\n\nRemember: Road vehicles yield to trains.", 108),
Topic("Queue beyond the railway", Category.RULES, "Wait before the crossing until your whole vehicle can clear it.\n\nNever enter a crossing if you risk stopping on the tracks.\n\nRemember: Open barriers do not make the space beyond sufficient.\n\nRemember: Any part of the car on the track is dangerous.\n\nRemember: That can trap your car on the crossing.", 110),
Topic("Home-zone speed", Category.RULES, "At walking speed.\n\nThe walking-speed rule applies even when no pedestrian is visible.\n\nRemember: A home zone is not an ordinary 30 zone.\n\nRemember: The home-zone rule replaces the ordinary limit.\n\nRemember: Walking speed remains the maximum.", 116),
Topic("Bicycle-street speed", Category.RULES, "Adapt to cyclists and never exceed 30 km/h.\n\nThe maximum is 30 km/h, but cyclists may require a lower speed.\n\nRemember: Adaptation may require a much lower speed.\n\nRemember: That is the home-zone rule, not the bicycle-street maximum.\n\nRemember: The 30 km/h maximum still applies.", 117),
Topic("Pedestrian-street access", Category.RULES, "No, ordinary through driving is not an authorised purpose.\n\nOnly specified essential access is allowed, such as certain deliveries or access to homes.\n\nRemember: Speed compliance alone does not authorise access.\n\nRemember: Absence of pedestrians does not permit through driving.\n\nRemember: The access restriction does not disappear at night.", 117),
Topic("Dipped and fog lights", Category.RULES, "No.\n\nThis combination is prohibited; use the permitted lighting appropriate to visibility.\n\nRemember: Darkness does not permit that combination.\n\nRemember: Vehicle age does not change the rule.\n\nRemember: Overtaking creates no lighting exception.", 262),
Topic("Signalling on a priority road", Category.RULES, "Yes, signal your change of direction.\n\nPriority does not replace communicating your intention to turn.\n\nRemember: The direction change still needs signalling.\n\nRemember: It is the manoeuvre that matters.\n\nRemember: That communicates the opposite direction.", 263),
Topic("Deep learning", Category.PERSONAL, "Understanding why rules exist and applying them to new situations.\n\nDeep learning connects rules to their purpose, helping you handle unfamiliar situations.\n\nRemember: Letters can change and do not teach the rule.\n\nRemember: You may miss the reasoning needed in a different situation.\n\nRemember: That leaves important gaps in knowledge.", 132),
Topic("Overlearning", Category.PERSONAL, "So more attention is available for traffic and hazards.\n\nWhen gear changing and other controls become familiar, they require less conscious effort.\n\nRemember: Observation remains essential.\n\nRemember: Practice cannot eliminate physical limits.\n\nRemember: Experience does not exempt a driver from rules.", 132),
Topic("Imitation learning", Category.PERSONAL, "Whether the behaviour being copied is safe and appropriate.\n\nImitation can teach good habits or reinforce dangerous ones.\n\nRemember: Car price does not prove good judgement.\n\nRemember: Confidence can exist alongside unsafe habits.\n\nRemember: Common behaviour is not necessarily safe or lawful.", 132),
Topic("Mature driving", Category.PERSONAL, "Wait calmly and leave them room to recover.\n\nTolerance reduces pressure and helps prevent an ordinary mistake becoming a dangerous situation.\n\nRemember: This adds stress rather than helping.\n\nRemember: Tailgating increases danger.\n\nRemember: Impatience does not justify an unsafe manoeuvre.", 132),
Topic("Overconfidence", Category.PERSONAL, "They may underestimate risk and overestimate their ability.\n\nLimited experience can produce confidence before reliable hazard judgement develops.\n\nRemember: It does not test every hazardous situation.\n\nRemember: Margins are still necessary.\n\nRemember: Human decisions remain a major risk.", 133),
Topic("Probability learning", Category.PERSONAL, "Continue checking for trains every time.\n\nA low past frequency does not make the next crossing safe.\n\nRemember: Rare events can still occur.\n\nRemember: Timetables and circumstances can change.\n\nRemember: Conditions may change before you cross.", 134),
Topic("Excessive stress", Category.PERSONAL, "Allow for the delay and continue driving calmly.\n\nExcessive stress can narrow attention and impair judgement; speeding adds risk.\n\nRemember: That increases collision risk.\n\nRemember: It does not make the journey safer.\n\nRemember: That draws attention away from current hazards.", 134),
Topic("Planning time", Category.PERSONAL, "Allow enough time and plan the route before setting off.\n\nA realistic schedule reduces pressure to rush or make sudden decisions.\n\nRemember: That creates pressure and risk.\n\nRemember: Last-second navigation increases distraction.\n\nRemember: This reduces safety margins rather than stress.", 134),
Topic("Negative peer pressure", Category.PERSONAL, "Keep a safe lawful speed and explain that safety comes first.\n\nThe driver remains responsible even when passengers pressure them.\n\nRemember: Pressure does not transfer the driver's duty.\n\nRemember: That is precisely the dangerous response.\n\nRemember: Passengers do not control your legal obligations.", 135),
Topic("Positive peer pressure", Category.PERSONAL, "Calmly asking the driver to slow down when driving unsafely.\n\nPassengers can support safer choices rather than encourage risk-taking.\n\nRemember: This competes with essential attention.\n\nRemember: That increases pressure to take risks.\n\nRemember: Silence can be interpreted as approval.", 135),
Topic("Alcohol threshold", Category.PERSONAL, "0.2 per mille.\n\nThe numerical threshold is 0.2 per mille in blood, corresponding to 0.1 mg per litre of exhaled air.\n\nRemember: Percent and per mille differ by a factor of ten.\n\nRemember: That is above Sweden's threshold.\n\nRemember: That is the numerical threshold associated with aggravated drink-driving.", 140),
Topic("Aggravated drink-driving threshold", Category.PERSONAL, "1.0 per mille.\n\nA level of at least 1.0 per mille is one criterion for aggravated drink-driving; other circumstances can also matter.\n\nRemember: That is not the aggravated numerical threshold.\n\nRemember: That is the ordinary numerical drink-driving threshold.\n\nRemember: Aggravated drink-driving can apply well below this level.", 140),
Topic("Alcohol breakdown", Category.PERSONAL, "None of these tricks; the body needs time.\n\nCoffee, showers and exercise do not provide a reliable shortcut to alcohol elimination.\n\nRemember: Feeling more alert does not remove alcohol faster.\n\nRemember: A shower does not accelerate the necessary metabolism.\n\nRemember: Exercise does not make you safely sober on demand.", 141),
Topic("The morning after", Category.PERSONAL, "Both remaining alcohol and impaired fitness from fatigue.\n\nFeeling hungover or sleep-deprived can impair driving even after alcohol levels decline.\n\nRemember: Smell cannot determine blood alcohol or fitness.\n\nRemember: Alertness does not establish sobriety or fitness.\n\nRemember: The amount consumed and time elapsed matter.", 141),
Topic("Prescribed medication", Category.PERSONAL, "Do not drive while impaired and seek advice about its effects.\n\nA prescription does not authorise driving when the medicine makes you unsafe.\n\nRemember: You remain responsible for safe driving.\n\nRemember: That can worsen impairment and is not driving advice.\n\nRemember: A short journey can still involve serious hazards.", 141),
Topic("Drugs and driving", Category.PERSONAL, "Overconfidence while fatigue may be masked.\n\nFeeling stimulated does not mean judgement and reaction are safe.\n\nRemember: Stimulation can distort risk assessment.\n\nRemember: Suppressed tiredness does not remove the need for sleep.\n\nRemember: No drug removes road hazards.", 142),
Topic("Handheld phone", Category.PERSONAL, "No.\n\nThe handheld-phone prohibition is not avoided by keeping the other hand on the wheel.\n\nRemember: Call duration does not create an exception.\n\nRemember: Holding it while using speaker mode is still handheld use.\n\nRemember: Familiarity does not remove distraction or the prohibition.", 143),
Topic("Hands-free distraction", Category.PERSONAL, "The conversation can take attention away from driving.\n\nFree hands do not guarantee an undistracted mind.\n\nRemember: Conversation still uses mental attention.\n\nRemember: The cognitive distraction is important.\n\nRemember: You must continue observing and responding to traffic.", 143),
Topic("Navigation preparation", Category.PERSONAL, "Before moving off, or after stopping somewhere safe and permitted.\n\nNavigation setup can divert eyes and attention from the road.\n\nRemember: A roundabout requires full attention.\n\nRemember: Overtaking requires careful observation.\n\nRemember: Traffic speed does not remove distraction.", 143),
Topic("Fatigue and legality", Category.PERSONAL, "Yes, if you cannot drive safely.\n\nFitness to drive includes fatigue, not just alcohol or illness.\n\nRemember: The safety duty still applies.\n\nRemember: There is no general short-trip exemption.\n\nRemember: All drivers must be fit to drive.", 148),
Topic("Fatigue warning signs", Category.PERSONAL, "Stop safely and address the tiredness.\n\nThese are warning signs that concentration and control may be deteriorating.\n\nRemember: Higher speed increases consequences and workload.\n\nRemember: Fresh air is not a reliable cure for fatigue.\n\nRemember: That reduces reaction margin when you need more of it.", 149),
Topic("Microsleep", Category.PERSONAL, "A brief unintended episode of sleep.\n\nEven a very brief loss of awareness can cover a substantial distance at road speed.\n\nRemember: Microsleep is a loss of wakefulness.\n\nRemember: Microsleep involves sleep rather than ordinary blinking.\n\nRemember: Any sleep while driving is dangerous.", 150),
Topic("Recovering from tiredness", Category.PERSONAL, "Adequate sleep.\n\nTemporary alertness measures do not replace restorative sleep.\n\nRemember: It cannot restore lost sleep.\n\nRemember: Cool air may feel refreshing but is not a reliable remedy.\n\nRemember: Willpower does not prevent involuntary sleep.", 150),
Topic("Central vision", Category.PERSONAL, "Reading the precise wording on a road sign.\n\nCentral vision provides the detail needed to identify text and fine features.\n\nRemember: That is mainly a peripheral-vision function.\n\nRemember: That uses hearing rather than vision.\n\nRemember: That uses physical sensations as well as sight.", 154),
Topic("Peripheral vision", Category.PERSONAL, "It helps detect movement and potential hazards outside your direct focus.\n\nYou can then turn your eyes to inspect the detected hazard more precisely.\n\nRemember: Central vision is better for fine detail.\n\nRemember: Both can impair visual attention.\n\nRemember: You still need active scanning.", 154),
Topic("Scanning ahead", Category.PERSONAL, "Move your gaze and scan both near and far ahead.\n\nFlexible scanning helps detect developing hazards before they become urgent.\n\nRemember: That narrows your information to one small area.\n\nRemember: Other road users and roadside activity matter too.\n\nRemember: You must also watch the road and surrounding traffic.", 155),
Topic("One headlight at night", Category.PERSONAL, "It may be a wider vehicle with a failed headlight.\n\nAssuming every single light is a narrow motorcycle can lead to inadequate clearance.\n\nRemember: Several vehicle types can appear as one light.\n\nRemember: That reduces clearance if the vehicle is wider than expected.\n\nRemember: The number of lights does not establish speed.", 156),
Topic("Tunnel vision", Category.PERSONAL, "Missing hazards at the sides of the road.\n\nStress, fatigue and alcohol can narrow attention to a smaller area.\n\nRemember: Tunnel vision reduces peripheral awareness.\n\nRemember: Narrowed awareness may delay hazard detection.\n\nRemember: Tunnel vision is an impairment, not protection.", 157),
Topic("Speed blindness", Category.PERSONAL, "Check the speedometer and adapt to the new road conditions.\n\nProlonged high speed can distort your subjective perception of speed.\n\nRemember: Your feeling may underestimate actual speed.\n\nRemember: Perception may be distorted.\n\nRemember: The changed feeling alone is not evidence of a fault.", 93),
Topic("Fossil carbon dioxide", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "It adds carbon dioxide from long-stored carbon to the atmosphere.\n\nCarbon dioxide contributes to the enhanced greenhouse effect.\n\nRemember: That is not the mechanism described.\n\nRemember: They are different compounds with different effects.\n\nRemember: Fuel-related greenhouse gas emissions are the relevant mechanism.", 304),
Topic("Catalytic converter warm-up", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Just after a cold engine is started.\n\nIt needs to reach operating temperature to treat exhaust effectively.\n\nRemember: Cold-start temperature, not the season alone, is the issue.\n\nRemember: A warmed converter normally works more effectively.\n\nRemember: Headlight use does not determine catalyst temperature.", 305),
Topic("Catalytic converter limits", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "No, fossil carbon dioxide emissions remain.\n\nConverters reduce several harmful pollutants but do not make fossil-fuel driving climate-neutral.\n\nRemember: Conventional converters do not do that.\n\nRemember: Warmth improves pollutant treatment, not elimination of fossil carbon.\n\nRemember: Speed does not turn it into a carbon-capture device.", 305),
Topic("Engine-heater timer", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "To warm the engine when needed without wasting electricity.\n\nExcessive heating time adds energy use without proportional benefit.\n\nRemember: That wastes electricity.\n\nRemember: Heating does not replace maintenance.\n\nRemember: The device is intended to warm the engine.", 306),
Topic("Roof-box resistance", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Remove it.\n\nEven an empty roof box increases aerodynamic drag and can increase consumption.\n\nRemember: Air resistance does not depend only on weight.\n\nRemember: That increases drag and is unsafe.\n\nRemember: Underinflation increases rolling resistance and harms handling.", 306),
Topic("Tyre pressure and energy", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "It increases rolling resistance and can increase fuel consumption.\n\nUse the manufacturer's recommended pressure for the load and conditions.\n\nRemember: Soft tyres generally require more energy to roll.\n\nRemember: Incorrect pressure can increase wear.\n\nRemember: Efficiency must not come from incorrect tyre settings.", 306),
Topic("Air conditioning", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Use it as needed for comfort, alertness and clear windows without unnecessary cooling.\n\nAir conditioning uses energy, but visibility and safe alertness take priority.\n\nRemember: Clear visibility is essential.\n\nRemember: That wastes energy.\n\nRemember: Discomfort and fatigue can harm safe driving.", 306),
Topic("Car washing", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "A purpose-built wash facility that treats wastewater.\n\nTreatment helps prevent oils and other residues reaching waterways.\n\nRemember: Polluted wash water can enter the environment.\n\nRemember: That puts contaminants close to the water.\n\nRemember: More detergent does not make untreated discharge harmless.", 307),
Topic("Carbon monoxide", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "It interferes with the blood's oxygen transport.\n\nCarbon monoxide can cause serious poisoning; exhaust must not accumulate around people.\n\nRemember: It has the opposite effect.\n\nRemember: Visibility does not establish safety.\n\nRemember: People are also vulnerable to poisoning.", 307),
Topic("Nitrogen oxides", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Airway irritation and contributions to acidification and ground-level ozone.\n\nNitrogen oxides are air pollutants with health and environmental effects.\n\nRemember: They contribute to pollution.\n\nRemember: They are different substances.\n\nRemember: Their effects concern air chemistry and health.", 307),
Topic("Traffic particles", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "They can harm the respiratory system.\n\nParticles arise from exhaust and wear, including tyres and road surfaces.\n\nRemember: Fine particles can be inhaled.\n\nRemember: Tyre and road wear still occur.\n\nRemember: Health effects are an important concern.", 308),
Topic("Traffic noise", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "A lower appropriate speed and smooth acceleration.\n\nNoise depends on both the engine and tyre-road interaction.\n\nRemember: That tends to increase noise.\n\nRemember: That creates unnecessary disturbance.\n\nRemember: Routine horn use is not a noise-reduction measure.", 308),
Topic("Efficient gear choice", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Use a suitably high gear while the engine runs smoothly.\n\nA high appropriate gear reduces engine speed; downshift if the engine labours.\n\nRemember: An unsuitable gear can make the engine labour.\n\nRemember: First gear is mainly for moving off.\n\nRemember: That wastes energy and can wear the clutch.", 312),
Topic("Looking ahead for efficiency", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Ease off early and prepare to stop smoothly.\n\nAnticipation avoids wasting energy accelerating towards a stop.\n\nRemember: That wastes energy and demands harder braking.\n\nRemember: That can compromise vehicle systems and control.\n\nRemember: Safety and traffic rules take priority.", 313),
Topic("Engine braking", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Slowing with the accelerator released while a suitable gear remains engaged.\n\nThe engine resists rotation; many fuel-injected cars cut fuel during overrun under suitable conditions.\n\nRemember: That disconnects the engine from the driven wheels.\n\nRemember: That can compromise control and is not the technique.\n\nRemember: That uses fuel without propelling the car.", 313),
Topic("Safety before economy", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Brake as needed to avoid a collision.\n\nEco-driving must never delay an essential safety response.\n\nRemember: That may not slow the car sufficiently.\n\nRemember: That disregards the pedestrian's safety.\n\nRemember: The urgent need is collision avoidance.", 314),
Topic("Electric-car emissions", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "It has no tailpipe exhaust while driving, but production and energy supply still have impacts.\n\nConsidering the full life cycle includes electricity, materials and manufacturing.\n\nRemember: Manufacturing and energy supply still matter.\n\nRemember: Tyres and road surfaces still wear.\n\nRemember: Battery manufacture requires materials and energy.", 318),
Topic("Hybrid power", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "It combines an electric motor with a combustion engine.\n\nThe two power sources can be used according to driving conditions and vehicle design.\n\nRemember: That is not the usual hybrid arrangement.\n\nRemember: A typical hybrid is not defined by diesel alone.\n\nRemember: An electric motor is part of the hybrid system.", 318),
Topic("Plug-in hybrid", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Its traction battery can be charged from an external electricity supply.\n\nExternal charging allows electric driving using energy obtained before the trip.\n\nRemember: It is still a hybrid.\n\nRemember: All vehicles need energy.\n\nRemember: That is not how external charging works.", 318),
Topic("Trip choice", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Walk or cycle instead of using the car.\n\nAvoiding the car trip avoids its fuel or traction-energy use.\n\nRemember: That adds energy use.\n\nRemember: That is unsafe and does not avoid the trip's emissions.\n\nRemember: That can increase drag.", 318),
Topic("Environmental zones", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Access may depend on the vehicle's emission class and the zone category.\n\nMunicipal zones can restrict specified vehicle types to improve local air quality.\n\nRemember: Some vehicles may be restricted.\n\nRemember: Fuel tax does not grant access.\n\nRemember: The registered vehicle characteristics matter, not appearance.", 319),
Topic("Emission classification", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Its registration information.\n\nThe registration data identifies the vehicle's relevant technical classification.\n\nRemember: Paint does not identify an emissions class.\n\nRemember: Seat count does not establish exhaust standards.\n\nRemember: The horn is unrelated to emissions classification.", 319),
Topic("Studs and air quality", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Studs contribute to road wear and airborne particles.\n\nLocal air-quality concerns can lead to signed restrictions.\n\nRemember: They can generate wear particles instead.\n\nRemember: Their purpose is traction in winter conditions.\n\nRemember: General seasonal rules and local restrictions are different.", 205),
Topic("Unnecessary weight", Category.ENVIRONMENT, "Remove it before future journeys.\n\nMoving unnecessary mass requires energy, especially during acceleration.\n\nRemember: Extra mass does not automatically improve efficiency.\n\nRemember: That adds drag and raises the centre of gravity.\n\nRemember: Unsecured cargo is a collision hazard.", 306),
Topic("Defensive driving", Category.SAFETY, "Anticipating mistakes and keeping safety margins.\n\nYou cannot control other people's behaviour, so leave room to respond.\n\nRemember: That can turn another person's mistake into a collision.\n\nRemember: That removes safety margins.\n\nRemember: People can misunderstand or make mistakes.", 7),
Topic("Speed and visibility", Category.SAFETY, "Slow enough to stop within the visible safe distance.\n\nThe posted limit is a maximum, not a guarantee that it is safe in current conditions.\n\nRemember: Visibility may require substantially less.\n\nRemember: That lengthens the distance needed to stop.\n\nRemember: A warning sound does not improve stopping ability.", 9),
Topic("Blind spots", Category.SAFETY, "A vehicle may be in a blind spot outside the mirrors' view.\n\nA final blind-spot check can reveal nearby road users before a sideways movement.\n\nRemember: They have coverage limits.\n\nRemember: Observation does not establish priority.\n\nRemember: Checking and signalling serve different purposes.", 17),
Topic("Waving pedestrians across", Category.SAFETY, "They may assume all lanes are safe when another vehicle is approaching.\n\nYou can control your own vehicle but cannot promise that other drivers will stop.\n\nRemember: Crossings are intended for them.\n\nRemember: Your gesture is not a police instruction.\n\nRemember: The risk is misleading reassurance, not brake performance.", 46),
Topic("Waiting to turn left", Category.SAFETY, "Keep them straight until you can turn.\n\nIf hit from behind, straight wheels reduce the risk of being pushed into oncoming traffic.\n\nRemember: A rear impact could push you across the centre line.\n\nRemember: That is not the normal waiting position.\n\nRemember: That obstructs oncoming traffic.", 79),
Topic("Risky country-road left turn", Category.SAFETY, "Continue to a suitable turning place and return for a right turn.\n\nAvoiding a hazardous waiting position can reduce rear-end and crossing risks.\n\nRemember: Following drivers may not react in time.\n\nRemember: You must not create that conflict.\n\nRemember: That introduces a dangerous manoeuvre.", 79),
Topic("Three-second gap", Category.SAFETY, "Count the time between the car ahead and your car passing the same fixed point.\n\nA time gap scales with speed; poor conditions require a larger margin.\n\nRemember: Three car lengths are insufficient at high speeds.\n\nRemember: That does not measure your following gap.\n\nRemember: Observation must continue while counting.", 81),
Topic("A tailgating driver", Category.SAFETY, "Increase your gap to the vehicle ahead and avoid sudden braking.\n\nMore space ahead lets you slow gradually, giving the following driver more time.\n\nRemember: That can trigger a rear-end collision.\n\nRemember: That leaves less time for a smooth response.\n\nRemember: That adds risk and does not solve the underlying problem.", 81),
Topic("Roadworks", Category.SAFETY, "Reduce speed and leave a generous margin.\n\nWorkers are exposed and temporary layouts can be unfamiliar.\n\nRemember: That increases danger in a constrained area.\n\nRemember: Temporary traffic arrangements may replace normal ones.\n\nRemember: People or equipment may be just beyond them.", 82),
Topic("Passing horses", Category.SAFETY, "Slow down, leave ample space and avoid sudden noise.\n\nHorses may react unpredictably to noise and nearby vehicles.\n\nRemember: That can frighten the horse.\n\nRemember: Sudden engine noise can provoke a reaction.\n\nRemember: That gives the animal little room and can startle it.", 83),
Topic("Overtaking decision", Category.SAFETY, "Enough visible clear distance to complete the manoeuvre safely.\n\nYou need space for the entire overtake, including a safe return to your lane.\n\nRemember: You also need to finish and return safely.\n\nRemember: Power alone does not provide visibility or a gap.\n\nRemember: A broken line does not make a blind overtake safe.", 98),
Topic("Being overtaken", Category.SAFETY, "Avoid accelerating and help create a safe outcome.\n\nYour duty to prevent danger remains even if the other driver breaks a rule.\n\nRemember: That prolongs the conflict.\n\nRemember: That can cause a collision.\n\nRemember: All drivers retain safety obligations.", 100),
Topic("Trapped on railway tracks", Category.SAFETY, "Get everyone away from the tracks and alert emergency services.\n\nProtect people first; call 112 and report the exact crossing and obstruction.\n\nRemember: Remaining in the vehicle can be fatal.\n\nRemember: That exposes you to another danger.\n\nRemember: The rail operator needs urgent warning through emergency services.", 110),
Topic("Hidden ice", Category.SAFETY, "The road may look safe, so drivers may not adapt in time.\n\nUnexpected low grip is dangerous when speed and steering are chosen for dry conditions.\n\nRemember: Thin ice can be difficult to see.\n\nRemember: Surface conditions can change locally.\n\nRemember: Tyres cannot remove the limits of grip.", 124),
Topic("Bridges in cold weather", Category.SAFETY, "Cold air can cool it from above and below.\n\nBridge surfaces can become icy even when nearby roads appear clear.\n\nRemember: That is not the reason for early freezing.\n\nRemember: Signals do not explain the temperature difference.\n\nRemember: Paved bridges can also ice over.", 125),
Topic("Snow smoke", Category.SAFETY, "Increase the following distance and reduce speed as visibility requires.\n\nSnow spray can hide traffic and road edges.\n\nRemember: That removes stopping margin.\n\nRemember: Hidden oncoming traffic may be present.\n\nRemember: Speed increases risk when visibility is poor.", 125),
Topic("Freezing rain", Category.SAFETY, "A nearly invisible slippery ice layer.\n\nRain can freeze on contact and dramatically reduce grip.\n\nRemember: On a cold surface it can create ice.\n\nRemember: Thin ice can be far more deceptive.\n\nRemember: A wet appearance may hide ice.", 125),
Topic("Seasonal slippery surfaces", Category.SAFETY, "Dirt and residues can make the surface unusually slippery.\n\nWater can mix with accumulated contaminants, reducing grip.\n\nRemember: Wet surfaces can be slippery in any season.\n\nRemember: Contaminants can initially worsen it.\n\nRemember: Loss of grip is not limited to ice.", 127),
Topic("A person with a white cane", Category.SAFETY, "Stop in good time and avoid confusing or startling them.\n\nA visually impaired pedestrian may rely strongly on sound and a predictable traffic situation.\n\nRemember: That can confuse or frighten them.\n\nRemember: They may not be able to see you.\n\nRemember: Waiting until they complete the crossing can avoid confusing engine noise.", 162),
Topic("Guide dogs", Category.SAFETY, "Avoid distracting it.\n\nThe dog is helping its handler navigate and needs to remain focused.\n\nRemember: That interrupts its work.\n\nRemember: It does not replace the handler's traffic judgement.\n\nRemember: Unnecessary noise can distract both dog and handler.", 163),
Topic("Children beside the road", Category.SAFETY, "The child may still move unexpectedly.\n\nChildren can act impulsively despite appearing to have noticed you.\n\nRemember: Eye contact is not a guarantee.\n\nRemember: Their abilities are still developing.\n\nRemember: Their next movement remains relevant.", 168),
Topic("Parked cars near a school", Category.SAFETY, "A small child may be completely hidden until stepping out.\n\nChildren's height and impulsive behaviour create a hazard that can appear suddenly.\n\nRemember: Priority does not remove the need for care.\n\nRemember: They may cross unpredictably.\n\nRemember: A horn is not a reliable safeguard.", 169),
Topic("Stopped school bus", Category.SAFETY, "Children may emerge from in front of or behind it.\n\nThe bus obscures your view and children may not check traffic reliably.\n\nRemember: The central danger is hidden children.\n\nRemember: That assumption is unsafe.\n\nRemember: Passengers can enter the road unexpectedly.", 169),
Topic("Arriving at a collision", Category.SAFETY, "Assess danger, protect people and call 112 with clear location details.\n\nA safe assessment helps avoid further casualties and gets suitable help to the scene.\n\nRemember: You could become another casualty.\n\nRemember: Immediate safety takes priority.\n\nRemember: Assistance may be urgently needed.", 175),
Topic("Dangerous-goods collision", Category.SAFETY, "Keep a safe distance and inform emergency services.\n\nLeaking substances may be toxic, flammable or explosive.\n\nRemember: That risks exposure.\n\nRemember: That can ignite flammable material.\n\nRemember: It can spread contamination or expose occupants.", 176),
Topic("Minor parking collision", Category.SAFETY, "Try to contact its owner and contact police if you cannot reach them.\n\nLeaving without dealing with the incident can constitute leaving the scene.\n\nRemember: Damage can be hidden and duties remain.\n\nRemember: The response should not be postponed.\n\nRemember: That does not inform the affected owner.", 177),
Topic("Wildlife risk", Category.SAFETY, "Around dusk and dawn near wildlife habitats and fence openings.\n\nAnimals can enter the road suddenly, particularly at common crossing points.\n\nRemember: Risk is not limited to that setting.\n\nRemember: Early anticipation helps prevent the collision.\n\nRemember: Animals can occur outside signed stretches too.", 177),
Topic("Wildlife collision reporting", Category.SAFETY, "Warn traffic, mark the collision location safely and notify police.\n\nRoe-deer collisions must be reported even when the animal leaves the scene.\n\nRemember: Injury may not be visible.\n\nRemember: That can hinder tracking and expose you to danger.\n\nRemember: Damage to your vehicle is not the reporting criterion.", 178),
Topic("Warning triangle", Category.SAFETY, "Use the warning triangle at a suitable distance where it gives early warning, if safe to place.\n\nDrivers need warning before reaching the obstruction; avoid putting yourself in traffic.\n\nRemember: It must be visible to approaching traffic.\n\nRemember: That exposes you to collision.\n\nRemember: Warning lights do not remove the need to seek safety.", 179),
Topic("Vision Zero", Category.SAFETY, "No one should be killed or seriously injured in road traffic.\n\nThe approach includes safer roads, vehicles and behaviour while recognising human mistakes.\n\nRemember: That is not the strategy.\n\nRemember: Road-system design also matters.\n\nRemember: Preventing severe harm to people is central.", 183),
Topic("Seat belt and airbag", Category.SAFETY, "No, they are designed to work together.\n\nThe belt helps position and restrain you so the airbag can provide appropriate protection.\n\nRemember: The belt is still necessary.\n\nRemember: Sitting too close adds risk.\n\nRemember: Drivers also need restraint.", 232),
Topic("Head restraint", Category.SAFETY, "To reduce damaging head movement in a collision.\n\nIt helps limit neck strain, especially in rear impacts.\n\nRemember: It does not restrain the whole body.\n\nRemember: It is a crash-safety feature.\n\nRemember: It does not affect tyre contact.", 234),
Topic("Rear-facing child restraint", Category.SAFETY, "They support the head and neck and distribute crash forces.\n\nA young child's relatively heavy head and vulnerable neck need suitable support.\n\nRemember: The restraint must still be installed correctly.\n\nRemember: That combination is dangerous.\n\nRemember: The restraint must fit the child and vehicle.", 238),
Topic("Child-restraint height rule", Category.SAFETY, "An appropriate approved child restraint.\n\nChildren shorter than 135 cm normally need a child seat or suitable booster restraint.\n\nRemember: An adult cannot safely restrain a child in a crash.\n\nRemember: A suitable child restraint is normally required below 135 cm.\n\nRemember: Rear-seat occupants also need protection.", 238),
Topic("Child seat and airbag", Category.SAFETY, "It must be deactivated.\n\nAn inflating frontal airbag can strike the rear-facing seat with fatal force.\n\nRemember: It creates a severe hazard in this arrangement.\n\nRemember: The danger is not limited to high speed.\n\nRemember: That does not neutralise airbag deployment.", 235),
Topic("Vehicle definition", Category.VEHICLE, "A person riding a bicycle.\n\nBicycles are vehicles even though they do not have engines.\n\nRemember: The definition also includes non-motorised vehicles.\n\nRemember: Rail-bound transport is excluded from this definition.\n\nRemember: A pedestrian is a road user but not operating a vehicle in this example.", 188),
Topic("Light-lorry speed", Category.VEHICLE, "The speed limit of the road, subject to safe adaptation.\n\nIt is not subject to the same general vehicle-specific cap as a heavy lorry.\n\nRemember: That is not a universal light-lorry limit.\n\nRemember: This confuses it with other vehicle rules.\n\nRemember: The road limit still applies.", 190),
Topic("Reaction versus braking", Category.VEHICLE, "Reaction distance.\n\nReaction distance covers the driver's response interval; braking distance begins when braking starts.\n\nRemember: That starts after the reaction interval.\n\nRemember: That is a physical vehicle dimension.\n\nRemember: No distance is travelled after it has stopped.", 196),
Topic("Braking-speed relationship", Category.VEHICLE, "It becomes approximately four times as long.\n\nBraking distance grows approximately with the square of speed.\n\nRemember: The car has much more kinetic energy.\n\nRemember: That describes the speed relationship for reaction distance at fixed reaction time.\n\nRemember: Higher speed does not shorten braking distance.", 198),
Topic("Tyres and reaction time", Category.VEHICLE, "No, but they can worsen the braking phase.\n\nReaction time is the human response interval; tyre grip affects what happens during braking.\n\nRemember: Tyres do not speed up the driver's mental response.\n\nRemember: There is no such direct relationship.\n\nRemember: Tyre condition is crucial for grip and stopping.", 202),
Topic("Summer tread depth", Category.VEHICLE, "1.6 mm.\n\nThe legal minimum is 1.6 mm, though wet performance can deteriorate before that minimum.\n\nRemember: That is below the permitted minimum.\n\nRemember: Three millimetres is the winter-condition requirement for relevant light vehicles.\n\nRemember: That is closer to new-tyre depth than the legal minimum.", 204),
Topic("Winter-tyre requirement", Category.VEHICLE, "1 December to 31 March.\n\nThe requirement depends on both the date interval and winter road conditions.\n\nRemember: That is not the specified requirement interval.\n\nRemember: Ice, slush and frost can also count.\n\nRemember: The requirement is not limited to country roads.", 205),
Topic("Winter tread depth", Category.VEHICLE, "3 mm.\n\nThe winter requirement is higher than the ordinary 1.6 mm minimum.\n\nRemember: That is below both relevant minima.\n\nRemember: That is insufficient for this winter-condition requirement.\n\nRemember: That is not the legal minimum.", 204),
Topic("Studded tyres outside the normal season", Category.VEHICLE, "Yes, when winter conditions prevail or are expected.\n\nThe seasonal prohibition has an exception for actual or expected winter road conditions.\n\nRemember: The winter-condition exception can apply.\n\nRemember: The exception is not based on motorway status.\n\nRemember: Time of day is not the criterion.", 205),
Topic("Tyre-pressure reference", Category.VEHICLE, "The vehicle's specified pressure information and tyre guidance.\n\nRecommended pressure depends on the vehicle, tyres and load.\n\nRemember: Vehicles have different requirements.\n\nRemember: Appearance does not reliably measure pressure.\n\nRemember: Bicycle and car tyres have different specifications.", 206),
Topic("Wheel imbalance", Category.VEHICLE, "Wheel imbalance.\n\nAn imbalanced rotating wheel can produce speed-dependent vibration and should be checked.\n\nRemember: Washer fluid does not normally cause this symptom.\n\nRemember: Indicator operation does not unbalance wheels.\n\nRemember: Clean glass has no such mechanical effect.", 207),
Topic("Temporary spare wheel", Category.VEHICLE, "The vehicle's limits for speed, distance and pressure.\n\nTemporary spares are intended for restricted use until the proper tyre is repaired or replaced.\n\nRemember: It may have important restrictions.\n\nRemember: Specifications differ.\n\nRemember: That may exceed its speed limit and increase risk.", 207),
Topic("Studded trailer tyres", Category.VEHICLE, "Studded tyres too.\n\nThe trailer must not have substantially poorer grip than the towing car under these rules.\n\nRemember: The condition still applies.\n\nRemember: That reverses the required relationship.\n\nRemember: A trailer's grip is essential for combination stability.", 208),
Topic("Best tyres at the rear", Category.VEHICLE, "To reduce the risk of the rear losing grip and swinging out.\n\nRear stability helps prevent oversteer and uncontrolled rotation.\n\nRemember: The aim is to reduce it.\n\nRemember: Front tyres contribute strongly to braking.\n\nRemember: They can lose contact with the road too.", 214),
Topic("Oversteer", Category.VEHICLE, "The rear loses grip and the car turns more than intended.\n\nLoss of rear grip can make the tail swing outward.\n\nRemember: That describes understeer.\n\nRemember: That is unrelated to the steering behaviour.\n\nRemember: Oversteer is itself a loss-of-control risk.", 214),
Topic("Understeer", Category.VEHICLE, "The front loses grip and the car turns less than intended.\n\nThe vehicle tends to continue straighter than the driver's intended curve.\n\nRemember: That describes oversteer.\n\nRemember: It can continue moving on an unintended path.\n\nRemember: Priority rules are unrelated to grip.", 216),
Topic("Electronic stability control", Category.VEHICLE, "Help stabilise the car using measures such as individual-wheel braking.\n\nIt can counter developing skids but cannot overcome every limit of grip.\n\nRemember: Physics still limits traction.\n\nRemember: Good tyres remain essential.\n\nRemember: No system guarantees that outcome.", 218),
Topic("Aquaplaning", Category.VEHICLE, "Water prevents the tyre from maintaining proper contact with the road.\n\nSteering and braking grip can be severely reduced when the tread cannot disperse water.\n\nRemember: Water can separate it from the road.\n\nRemember: Aquaplaning is a tyre-road contact problem.\n\nRemember: That is unrelated to the phenomenon.", 219),
Topic("Aquaplaning response", Category.VEHICLE, "Ease off the accelerator and avoid abrupt steering or braking.\n\nSmooth inputs help the wheels recover grip without a sudden change in direction.\n\nRemember: Abrupt braking can destabilise the car when grip returns.\n\nRemember: Sudden steering increases loss-of-control risk.\n\nRemember: More speed worsens the tendency to float on water.", 219),
Topic("Dual brake circuits", Category.VEHICLE, "To retain some braking if one circuit fails.\n\nSeparating the circuits reduces the chance that a single failure removes all braking.\n\nRemember: Hydraulic circuits still use fluid.\n\nRemember: Performance can be seriously reduced.\n\nRemember: The driver still controls normal braking.", 224),
Topic("ABS purpose", Category.VEHICLE, "It helps preserve steering control by preventing sustained wheel lock.\n\nABS regulates brake pressure so tyres can continue rotating and maintain useful steering grip.\n\nRemember: On some surfaces the distance can be longer.\n\nRemember: The driver must still apply the brakes.\n\nRemember: Stopping distance and reaction time still exist.", 224),
Topic("ABS pedal pulsation", Category.VEHICLE, "Maintain firm pressure and steer as needed.\n\nPulsation is a normal sign of ABS pressure regulation during hard braking.\n\nRemember: That can reduce the braking effort you need.\n\nRemember: The system already modulates pressure.\n\nRemember: That is not the normal braking response.", 225),
Topic("Sinking brake pedal", Category.VEHICLE, "A potentially serious brake-system leak or fault.\n\nLoss of hydraulic pressure requires prompt professional attention.\n\nRemember: Continuing to sink is a warning sign.\n\nRemember: Fuel level does not explain hydraulic pressure loss.\n\nRemember: Speed will not repair faulty brakes.", 225),
Topic("Spongy brake pedal", Category.VEHICLE, "Air in the brake system.\n\nAir compresses, unlike the intended hydraulic pressure transmission, producing a spongy feel.\n\nRemember: That system is separate.\n\nRemember: New tyres do not explain a spongy hydraulic pedal.\n\nRemember: Window position does not affect the brake circuit.", 225),
Topic("Crumple zones", Category.VEHICLE, "To absorb energy and reduce the forces reaching occupants.\n\nControlled deformation extends the deceleration process while the passenger cell protects space.\n\nRemember: An abrupt stop produces greater forces.\n\nRemember: Belts remain essential.\n\nRemember: The occupied space should remain protected.", 232),
Topic("Securing luggage", Category.VEHICLE, "Low down and secured against suitable supports in the luggage area.\n\nA low centre of gravity and restraint reduce movement during braking or collision.\n\nRemember: It can become a projectile.\n\nRemember: That raises the centre of gravity.\n\nRemember: A person cannot reliably hold it in a crash.", 252),
Topic("Gross weight", Category.VEHICLE, "The vehicle's actual weight at a particular time.\n\nIt includes what the vehicle currently carries rather than only its registered limit.\n\nRemember: That is a limit, not necessarily the actual current weight.\n\nRemember: Gross weight covers the whole vehicle and current load.\n\nRemember: It is not limited to a trailer or empty condition.", 252),
Topic("Total weight", Category.VEHICLE, "Unladen weight plus maximum permitted load.\n\nIt is the registered maximum loaded weight, distinct from actual gross weight.\n\nRemember: That is only part of a load.\n\nRemember: That describes gross weight.\n\nRemember: Those quantities do not define weight.", 252),
Topic("Light-trailer exception", Category.VEHICLE, "Yes, under the trailer-not-exceeding-750-kg rule.\n\nA trailer up to 750 kg can be towed on ordinary B even when combined total weights exceed 3,500 kg; technical towing limits still apply.\n\nRemember: The 750 kg trailer exception exists.\n\nRemember: Technical limits must still be respected.\n\nRemember: The licence rule here uses registered total weights.", 254),
Topic("Technical towing limit", Category.VEHICLE, "The car's technical towing limit as well as the licence limits.\n\nA broader licence does not make a vehicle capable of towing more than its approval allows.\n\nRemember: The car's technical limit remains binding.\n\nRemember: Colour does not establish towing capacity.\n\nRemember: Terrain does not remove the vehicle limit.", 255),
Topic("Excessive towball load", Category.VEHICLE, "The car's front axle can be unloaded, impairing steering grip.\n\nToo much downward coupling force can lift the front relative to the rear.\n\nRemember: The load transfer can reduce their grip.\n\nRemember: The car's attitude can change and dazzle others.\n\nRemember: The coupling transmits forces to it.", 256),
Topic("Protruding load at night", Category.VEHICLE, "With a red light and red reflectors.\n\nRearward markings use red; forward markings use white.\n\nRemember: That is not the required rear marking colour.\n\nRemember: It may be invisible in darkness.\n\nRemember: That is not ordinary load marking.", 247),
Topic("Daytime running lights in bad visibility", Category.VEHICLE, "Select the proper lights so the rear and other required lights are on.\n\nSome cars need manual switching when daylight visibility worsens.\n\nRemember: The system may not respond correctly.\n\nRemember: Being visible is a safety requirement.\n\nRemember: It does not provide the required exterior lighting.", 264),
Topic("Pre-drive safety check", Category.VEHICLE, "To detect faults that could make the vehicle unsafe.\n\nA brief inspection can identify defects before they cause a traffic problem.\n\nRemember: The main purpose is safe function.\n\nRemember: Checks do not replace servicing.\n\nRemember: Different systems need separate checks.", 272),
Topic("Driving position", Category.VEHICLE, "Before moving off.\n\nA stable position and correct mirror view help you operate controls and observe traffic.\n\nRemember: That adds distraction at a demanding moment.\n\nRemember: You should prepare before encountering hazards.\n\nRemember: Different drivers need different settings.", 273),
Topic("First inspection", Category.VEHICLE, "No later than 36 months after the month it first entered service.\n\nThe first interval is 36 months, followed by a second interval of 24 months.\n\nRemember: Fourteen months applies to subsequent intervals after the second inspection.\n\nRemember: Inspection has a deadline independent of symptoms.\n\nRemember: That is far later than the first deadline.", 279),
Topic("Later inspections", Category.VEHICLE, "14 months after the month of the previous inspection.\n\nThe continuing schedule differs from the initial 36- and 24-month intervals.\n\nRemember: That is the initial interval for a new car.\n\nRemember: That is the second interval.\n\nRemember: Ownership transfer does not replace the periodic schedule.", 279),
Topic("Missed inspection", Category.VEHICLE, "An automatic driving ban applies until the inspection requirements are addressed.\n\nOwners are responsible for meeting the deadline even without a reminder.\n\nRemember: Off-road status is a separate notification.\n\nRemember: A reminder is not required for the deadline to apply.\n\nRemember: Apparent condition does not remove the inspection requirement.", 280),
Topic("Electrical fuse", Category.VEHICLE, "To protect a circuit against excessive current.\n\nA fuse interrupts an overloaded circuit to reduce damage and fire risk.\n\nRemember: That is the opposite of its protective role.\n\nRemember: Oil provides lubrication.\n\nRemember: A fuse does not store electrical energy.", 284),
Topic("Hot coolant system", Category.VEHICLE, "Pressurised hot coolant or steam can cause burns.\n\nAllow the system to cool and follow the vehicle's instructions.\n\nRemember: It may be dangerously hot.\n\nRemember: It does not establish or fix the cause.\n\nRemember: That is unrelated to the cooling system.", 285),
Topic("Oil-pressure warning", Category.VEHICLE, "Stop safely and investigate according to the vehicle instructions before continuing.\n\nInadequate oil pressure can cause severe engine damage.\n\nRemember: Continued operation may damage the engine.\n\nRemember: That does not correct oil pressure.\n\nRemember: Hiding the warning does not resolve the fault.", 285),
Topic("Ownership document", Category.VEHICLE, "It contains ownership information and is used for ownership-related procedures.\n\nIt should not be casually left accessible inside the car.\n\nRemember: It has no propulsion function.\n\nRemember: Vehicle ownership and driving entitlement are different.\n\nRemember: Inspections are separate events.", 292),
Topic("Off-road notification", Category.VEHICLE, "No, off-road registration requires a separate notification.\n\nA driving ban and registered off-road status are different.\n\nRemember: The ban does not make the off-road notification.\n\nRemember: Non-use alone does not register its status.\n\nRemember: Fuel level is unrelated to registration status.", 293),
Topic("Third-party insurance", Category.VEHICLE, "Damage to your own vehicle.\n\nThird-party cover addresses personal injury and damage to other parties rather than own-car damage.\n\nRemember: Personal injury is central to third-party cover.\n\nRemember: Third-party property damage is a main purpose.\n\nRemember: That is the type of damage it generally covers.", 298),
)
